## Quick start

> **To write:** the shortest path from nothing to {{ 'a program that uses it' if primary == 'library' else 'it working' }}, as numbered steps with
> commands to copy. Start with what the reader needs, and end at something they can see work.
> Depth, such as every option or platform, goes on a page under `docs/`, with an entry under
> Documentation. When what the reader needs, or where its files live and how to remove it, runs
> past a few lines, give it a section of its own between Quick start and Documentation, such as
> `## Requirements`, and add it to `readme_sections` in `Cargo.toml`. For this
> {{ label[primary] }}, cover:
>
{%- if primary == 'web' %}
> - where it listens, and how a stranger reaches the UI: the bind address, a proxy or tunnel, and
>   any feature that needs HTTPS;
> - how the first account is made, if it has accounts;
> - the outside systems its pages need;
{%- elif primary == 'desktop' %}
> - the supported OS versions, and the install form: an installer, a zip or a dmg;
> - whether it's signed, and the warning a user sees if not, such as SmartScreen or Gatekeeper;
> - first-run steps, such as permissions and sign-ins;
> - where it keeps settings and data, and what uninstalling leaves;
> - whether it updates itself, and how a user learns it did;
> - tray or background behaviour, and the shortcuts that matter;
{%- elif primary == 'terminal' %}
> - the system and the shell the commands are for;
> - the minimum terminal size, and the terminals it's tested in;
> - where its window goes if it runs beside something else;
> - outside accounts or profiles its data depends on;
> - a demo or offline mode, if it has one, as the first check that it runs;
> - whether it's signed, and the warning a user sees if not;
> - whether it updates itself;
> - the keys that matter in the first minute;
{%- elif primary == 'cli' %}
> - the install forms, and the shell the commands are for;
> - what it needs first, such as a token or a config file, and where that lives;
> - whether it's signed, and the warning a user sees if not;
> - output for scripts, such as exit codes or JSON, if a page doesn't cover it;
{%- elif primary == 'service' %}
> - how it's reached: its port, its authentication, and any proxy in front;
> - the configuration that matters: the environment variables, and where its data persists;
> - what it needs to run, such as memory, disk or outside services;
{%- else %}
> - the dependency line, below;
> - the minimum Rust version, which `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml` sets, and any `no_std` or
>   async runtime needs;
> - a whole program that calls it, if the example above isn't one;
> - what [Stability](docs/stability.md) promises, and what 1.0 would mean;
{%- endif %}
{%- for other in others %}
> - its {{ label[other] }}: {{ {'web': 'where its UI listens, how to reach it, and the page the reader sees first;', 'desktop': 'how to start it, and its first window;', 'terminal': 'how to start it, and its first screen;', 'cli': 'the command a person runs first, and what it prints;', 'service': 'how to start it in the background, and how to check that it runs;'}[other] }}
{%- endfor %}
> - the last step: {{ {'web': 'open the page, and what the reader sees there.', 'desktop': 'the first window, and what the reader sees in it.', 'terminal': 'the first screen, and what the reader sees on it.', 'cli': 'the first command, and what it prints.', 'service': 'a health check, or a request and its response.', 'library': 'run the program, and what it prints.'}[primary] }}
